---
name: sports-live-alerts
description: >-
  Engine for Sports Alerts. Use to install or repair the live-score watcher on
  this computer, arm or stop it for a game, deliver its events (webhook wake or
  backup run), list fixtures, or explain what sports and events are supported.
---
# Sports live alerts (engine)

A small Python watcher on this computer follows live games and wakes you only when something happens. Polling costs no credits. Each pushed event is one wake (one short message). The 30-min backup runs also wake you, so keep them to game windows.

## Supported feeds
- **ESPN public JSON** (no key, unofficial, can change without notice). Path is `sport/league`:
  soccer: `eng.1` `esp.1` `ita.1` `ger.1` `fra.1` `usa.1` `uefa.champions` `uefa.europa` `fifa.world` `eng.fa` …; `basketball/nba` `basketball/wnba`; `football/nfl` `football/college-football`; `baseball/mlb`; `hockey/nhl`. Other ESPN league slugs usually work too. Test one with `curl -s https://site.api.espn.com/apis/site/v2/sports/<sport>/<league>/scoreboard`.
- **F1**: F1's own live timing (livetiming.formula1.com, SignalR). Calendar from api.jolpi.ca. Race and Sprint only.

## Events (kinds)
ESPN: `start` (game underway or watcher joined mid-game), `score` (goal/TD/run with the scorer from ESPN details or the summary), `red` (soccer red card), `period` (half-time, end of quarter or period, extra time/OT), `final` (also POSTPONED). Basketball defaults to start, period, final (scoring is too frequent). Baseball defaults to start, score, final.
F1: `start` `lead` `positions` (top-10 moves only) `track` (SC/VSC/red flag/green) `incident` `retired` `result` (top 10).
Every change fires on the first poll that sees it (fast mode); ESPN occasionally flickers, which shows up as a quick "Score corrected". A soccer goal names the scorer only once ESPN's details have caught up with the score; otherwise it goes out with just the score. A score that goes down fires as "Score corrected" (VAR or review).

## Files (all under /home/box/sportsalerts; /home survives restarts)
`config.json` follows, `common.py` helpers, `espn.py` ESPN watcher (polls every 10 s while live, every 60 s pre-game, exits when no followed game is live or starting within 35 min), `f1.py` F1 watcher (only connects within 35 min before to 3.5 h after a GP or Sprint), `schedule.py` fixtures and cron listeners, `report.py` unsent events, `start.sh` idempotent starter. Runtime files: `events.jsonl`, `sent_id`, `state_espn.json`, `status_*.json`, `watcher.log`.

config.json:
```json
{"tz":"Europe/London","quiet_hours":["23:30","07:30"],
 "follows":[{"sport":"soccer","league":"eng.1","team":"Arsenal","events":["score","red","final"]},
            {"sport":"basketball","league":"nba","team":"LAL"},
            {"sport":"soccer","league":"uefa.champions"},
            {"sport":"f1","events":["start","lead","track","result"]}]}
```
`team` takes an ESPN team id, abbreviation, or name (whole-word match). Omit it to follow the whole league. `game` takes one ESPN event id. Omit `events` to use the defaults. `quiet_hours` set to null means always on. During quiet hours pushes are held, and report.py prints `QUIET` until quiet hours end.

## Install or repair
Run every block below in Shell, then run `pip install --user websockets` (needed for F1 only) and `chmod +x /home/box/sportsalerts/*.py /home/box/sportsalerts/start.sh`. Never overwrite an existing config.json. Self-test: `cd /home/box/sportsalerts && python3 schedule.py 3` should list fixtures.

